1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 6.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:05,850
1, Group Policy Basics, the start of
Learning Plan 6.

3
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:12,100
Now you make every computer and user
behave the way you want, from one place.

4
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:17,150
You will learn what Group Policy does and
where a GPO lives.

5
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:23,000
You will tell computer settings from user
settings, and predict which GPO wins with

6
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:25,250
LSDOU, link order and precedence.

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,700
You will use Block Inheritance and
Enforced, create and link GPOs in the

8
00:00:30,950 --> 00:00:36,150
console and in PowerShell, and prove the
result with gpupdate, gpresult and Group

9
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:37,600
Policy Modeling.

10
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:43,450
Group Policy lets you set a setting once,
in Active Directory, and every computer

11
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:46,900
and user in scope applies it by itself.

12
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:51,150
The setting lives in a Group Policy
Object, or GPO.

13
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:54,200
A GPO on its own does nothing.

14
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:58,850
You link it to a site, the domain or an
OU.

15
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:05,100
Link SA1 Student Desktop to the Students
OU, and Dev, Erin and every new student

16
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:06,550
get it.

17
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:11,600
For the Server+ exam, think centralized
configuration: one baseline, and drift is

18
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:13,850
fixed at the next refresh.

19
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:20,100
Every GPO is stored in two halves, and
both carry the same ID, a GUID.

20
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:25,950
The Group Policy container is an object in
Active Directory, under Policies in the

21
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:27,400
System container.

22
00:01:27,650 --> 00:01:30,450
It holds the name, version and status.

23
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:35,900
The Group Policy template is a folder in
SYSVOL on every domain controller.

24
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:42,950
It holds a file called GPT dot INI and the
Machine and User folders with the actual

25
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:44,400
settings.

26
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:47,050
The link is a third piece.

27
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:54,900
It is stored on the site, domain or OU,
which is why one GPO can be linked in many

28
00:01:55,150 --> 00:01:56,350
places.

29
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,000
Every GPO has two halves inside.

30
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:03,250
Computer Configuration applies to computer
objects in the linked OU.

31
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:08,300
Windows applies it when the computer
starts, and then in the background.

32
00:02:08,550 --> 00:02:12,150
The logon message on CLIENT01 is a
computer setting.

33
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:18,400
User Configuration applies to user objects
in the linked OU, at sign-in and then in

34
00:02:18,650 --> 00:02:19,850
the background.

35
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:26,100
Blocking Control Panel for dpatel is a
user setting, and it follows him to any

36
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:27,550
PC.

37
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:29,800
Each half has two nodes.

38
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:34,450
Policies are enforced, and they disappear
when the GPO stops applying.

39
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:37,100
Preferences only set a starting value.

40
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:41,350
Administrative Templates are the thousands
of registry-based settings, such as

41
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:43,600
Prohibit access to Control Panel.

42
00:02:43,850 --> 00:02:49,850
ADMX files define the settings, and ADML
files hold the words for a language, like

43
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:51,300
English.

44
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:56,350
Each setting writes a value under a
Policies key in the registry.

45
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:02,600
With no central store, which is how our
pod starts, the editor reads the templates

46
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:06,850
from C Windows PolicyDefinitions on the
computer you work on.

47
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:11,900
A central store is one PolicyDefinitions
folder in SYSVOL that every administrator

48
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:13,350
uses.

49
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:19,200
New templates are a download from
Microsoft, and our pod has no internet, so

50
00:03:19,450 --> 00:03:21,450
we use the built-in ones.

51
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:23,700
When does a setting arrive?

52
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:26,750
Computer Configuration applies when the
computer starts.

53
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,200
User Configuration applies when the user
signs in.

54
00:03:30,450 --> 00:03:36,050
After that, Windows checks again in the
background every 90 minutes, plus a random

55
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:43,100
extra of up to 30 minutes, so that
hundreds of clients do not all ask at the

56
00:03:43,350 --> 00:03:44,550
same moment.

57
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:47,200
Domain controllers check every 5 minutes.

58
00:03:47,450 --> 00:03:53,450
So in the worst case a change takes about
two hours to reach a client.

59
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:58,100
If you cannot wait, run gpupdate dash
force on the client.

60
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:03,950
GPOs are applied in a fixed order, and you
can remember it as LSDOU.

61
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:07,000
First the local policy on the computer.

62
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:09,650
Then GPOs linked to the site.

63
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:13,500
Then the domain, where the Default Domain
Policy lives.

64
00:04:13,750 --> 00:04:16,950
Then the OUs, parent first and child last.

65
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:23,200
When two GPOs set the same setting in
different ways, the one applied last wins.

66
00:04:23,450 --> 00:04:29,850
That means the OU closest to the object
wins, unless a link higher up is Enforced.

67
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:33,700
For Dev Patel, that is the link on
Students.

68
00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:35,950
By default, settings flow down.

69
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:41,400
A GPO linked to Lakeshore reaches
Students, Staff and every other child OU.

70
00:04:41,650 --> 00:04:42,850
That is inheritance.

71
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:46,300
Block Inheritance is a setting on an OU.

72
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:53,350
Turn it on for Staff and GPOs linked above
Staff no longer reach it, and GPMC marks

73
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,400
the OU with a blue exclamation mark.

74
00:04:56,650 --> 00:04:59,450
Enforced is a setting on a link.

75
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:04,900
An enforced GPO cannot be overridden by
lower links, and it passes straight

76
00:05:05,150 --> 00:05:06,350
through Block Inheritance.

77
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:10,600
So the rule to remember is simple:
Enforced beats Block.

78
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:15,650
When several GPOs are linked to the same
OU, link order decides.

79
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:20,300
Link order 1 is applied last, so it wins a
conflict.

80
00:05:20,550 --> 00:05:26,150
To see the full picture for an OU, open
its Group Policy Inheritance tab.

81
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:31,200
It lists every GPO that reaches the OU,
and precedence 1 wins.

82
00:05:31,450 --> 00:05:34,250
Every domain also has two default GPOs.

83
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:40,100
The Default Domain Policy is linked to the
domain and holds password, lockout and

84
00:05:40,350 --> 00:05:41,550
Kerberos policy.

85
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:45,400
The Default Domain Controllers Policy
covers the domain controllers.

86
00:05:45,650 --> 00:05:50,850
Leave them for those jobs and create a new
GPO for everything else.

87
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:56,300
Your main tool is the Group Policy
Management Console, or GPMC, from Server

88
00:05:56,550 --> 00:05:57,750
Manager's Tools menu.

89
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,400
Expand the forest, Domains and sa1.

90
00:06:00,650 --> 00:06:04,650
lab, and you see the OU tree with its
links.

91
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:10,100
To create and link in one step,
right-click the OU, here Students, and

92
00:06:10,350 --> 00:06:14,750
choose Create a GPO in this domain, and
Link it here.

93
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,600
Name the GPO for its job, SA1 Student
Desktop.

94
00:06:18,850 --> 00:06:22,050
Block Inheritance is on the same
right-click menu.

95
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:25,900
Right-click a link for Edit, Enforced and
Link Enabled.

96
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:30,550
Edit opens the Group Policy Management
Editor for that one GPO.

97
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:34,800
Under User Configuration, Policies, you
find Administrative Templates, and the

98
00:06:35,050 --> 00:06:40,650
label tells you the templates come from
the local computer, because there is no

99
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:42,100
central store yet.

100
00:06:42,350 --> 00:06:47,150
Open Control Panel and double-click
Prohibit access to Control Panel and PC

101
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:48,600
settings.

102
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:50,850
Choose Enabled and select OK.

103
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:53,100
There is no Save button.

104
00:06:53,350 --> 00:06:59,350
The GPO changes in Active Directory and
SYSVOL right away, and clients pick it up

105
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:01,200
at their next refresh.

106
00:07:01,450 --> 00:07:05,050
The GroupPolicy module does the same work
in PowerShell.

107
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:11,300
New-GPO creates an empty GPO, and you can
pipe it straight into New-GPLink with the

108
00:07:11,550 --> 00:07:12,750
target OU.

109
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,200
Set-GPRegistryValue can write
registry-based settings, like the Control

110
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:20,050
Panel setting, but not every setting has a
cmdlet.

111
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:25,100
Security options such as the logon message
are set in the editor.

112
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:29,750
Finally, Get-GPInheritance shows an OU's
own links, whether it blocks inheritance,

113
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,800
and the inherited links in precedence
order.

114
00:07:33,050 --> 00:07:37,050
That last line is what a client in
Students gets.

115
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:40,500
Here is how those ideas look in GPMC.

116
00:07:40,750 --> 00:07:45,950
Right-click an OU, such as Staff, and
choose Block Inheritance, and the blue

117
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:47,400
exclamation mark appears.

118
00:07:47,650 --> 00:07:52,450
Right-click a link, such as SA1 Student
Desktop under Lakeshore, and choose

119
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:55,500
Enforced, and a padlock appears on it.

120
00:07:55,750 --> 00:07:59,750
Now select Students and open the Group
Policy Inheritance tab.

121
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,600
The enforced link from Lakeshore sits at
precedence 1, above the link on Students

122
00:08:05,850 --> 00:08:09,050
itself, because nothing can override an
enforced GPO.

123
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:11,700
In PowerShell, use Set-GPInheritance and
Set-GPLink.

124
00:08:11,950 --> 00:08:14,350
Always test on a real client.

125
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:18,600
On CLIENT01, signed in as dpatel, run
gpupdate dash force.

126
00:08:18,850 --> 00:08:22,050
Then run gpresult slash r slash scope
user.

127
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:27,100
It shows the user's distinguished name,
the domain controller that delivered the

128
00:08:27,350 --> 00:08:32,950
policy, and the list of applied GPOs,
where you should see SA1 Student Desktop.

129
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:37,600
For the full picture, gpresult slash h
writes an HTML report.

130
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:43,450
One catch: the computer part of gpresult
needs an administrator window, so sign in

131
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:45,700
as an admin for that.

132
00:08:45,950 --> 00:08:48,350
GPMC has two wizards for checking.

133
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:54,200
Group Policy Results reads what really
applied on a computer for a user who

134
00:08:54,450 --> 00:08:56,850
signed in there, much like gpresult.

135
00:08:57,100 --> 00:09:02,300
Group Policy Modeling asks a domain
controller what would apply for any user

136
00:09:02,550 --> 00:09:07,350
and computer, before you change anything,
so it is perfect for planning.

137
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:11,200
Its Advanced Simulation Options page even
simulates loopback processing.

138
00:09:11,450 --> 00:09:16,650
Loopback makes user settings follow the
computer, which suits shared lab PCs or

139
00:09:16,900 --> 00:09:18,100
Remote Desktop servers.

140
00:09:18,350 --> 00:09:22,350
Merge adds the computer's user settings to
the user's own.

141
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:24,600
Replace uses only the computer's.

142
00:09:24,850 --> 00:09:26,050
Four common mistakes.

143
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:30,700
One: linking a GPO with user settings to
the Workstations OU.

144
00:09:30,950 --> 00:09:37,350
User settings follow user objects, so link
it where the users are, or use loopback on

145
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:38,800
purpose.

146
00:09:39,050 --> 00:09:42,250
Two: putting everything into the Default
Domain Policy.

147
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:48,500
Keep it for password, lockout and Kerberos
policy, and create one new GPO per job.

148
00:09:48,750 --> 00:09:50,350
Three: expecting changes instantly.

149
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:56,600
Wait for the refresh or run gpupdate dash
force, and prove the result with gpresult.

150
00:09:56,850 --> 00:09:59,650
Four: using Block Inheritance and Enforced
everywhere.

151
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:01,900
Fix the OU design first.

152
00:10:02,150 --> 00:10:05,350
Time to check yourself, using the
Lakeshore tree.

153
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:10,400
First, a GPO with only User Configuration
settings is linked to Workstations.

154
00:10:10,650 --> 00:10:15,050
Does dpatel get those settings when he
signs in to CLIENT01?

155
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:21,300
Second, the Default Domain Policy and a
GPO on Students set the same setting in

156
00:10:21,550 --> 00:10:22,750
opposite ways.

157
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Which one wins for dpatel?

158
00:10:25,250 --> 00:10:30,050
Third, Staff blocks inheritance, and a GPO
linked to Lakeshore is Enforced.

159
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:31,900
Does it reach cdiaz?

160
00:10:32,150 --> 00:10:38,150
Fourth, you changed a GPO ten minutes ago,
and CLIENT01 does not show it yet.

161
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,000
What do you run?

162
00:10:40,250 --> 00:10:42,250
Pause the video and answer.

163
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:44,100
Here are the answers.

164
00:10:44,350 --> 00:10:45,550
One: no.

165
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:51,000
Dev Patel's user object is in Students,
not Workstations, and user settings follow

166
00:10:51,250 --> 00:10:54,050
the user, unless loopback is turned on.

167
00:10:54,300 --> 00:10:56,700
Two: the GPO on Students wins.

168
00:10:56,950 --> 00:11:03,350
In LSDOU order the OU is applied after the
domain, so it has the last word.

169
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:04,800
Three: yes.

170
00:11:05,050 --> 00:11:09,450
Enforced passes through Block Inheritance,
while the non-enforced GPOs from above

171
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:10,900
stay blocked.

172
00:11:11,150 --> 00:11:17,150
Four: run gpupdate dash force on CLIENT01,
then gpresult slash r, and look for the

173
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:21,000
GPO in the list of applied Group Policy
Objects.

174
00:11:21,250 --> 00:11:24,050
Let me show you on the pod.

175
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:29,500
In GPMC on DC01, I find the two default
GPOs and their links.

176
00:11:29,750 --> 00:11:35,350
I create SA1 Student Desktop on the
Students OU and enable the Control Panel

177
00:11:35,600 --> 00:11:36,800
setting.

178
00:11:37,050 --> 00:11:42,650
Then PowerShell creates the logon message
GPO and links it to Workstations, and I

179
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:46,100
set the title and text in the editor.

180
00:11:46,350 --> 00:11:52,350
On CLIENT01 I run gpupdate dash force and
sign out, and the message appears before

181
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:53,800
sign-in.

182
00:11:54,050 --> 00:11:55,250
No restart needed.

183
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:59,900
As dpatel, Control Panel is blocked, and
gpresult names the GPO.

184
00:12:00,150 --> 00:12:06,150
Finally I link the GPO to Lakeshore, block
Staff, enforce the link, and read the

185
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:07,600
precedence list.

186
00:12:07,850 --> 00:12:09,050
To wrap up.

187
00:12:09,300 --> 00:12:15,700
A GPO is a container in Active Directory
plus a template in SYSVOL, and links put

188
00:12:15,950 --> 00:12:17,150
it to work.

189
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:21,000
Computer settings follow computers and
user settings follow users.

190
00:12:21,250 --> 00:12:27,250
LSDOU sets the order, the closest OU wins,
link order 1 wins, and Enforced beats

191
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:28,700
Block Inheritance.

192
00:12:28,950 --> 00:12:34,150
Leave the default GPOs for their jobs and
make one GPO per job.

193
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:39,200
Refresh with gpupdate dash force and prove
it with gpresult or Modeling.

194
00:12:39,450 --> 00:12:40,650
In Lab 6.

195
00:12:40,900 --> 00:12:46,500
1 you do all of this on DC01 and CLIENT01,
in about 75 minutes.
